WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.000
 There is very little in valuation that I think of as new, different or sophisticated.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.000
 Much of what we do has always been done.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.000
 This session is an exception.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:26.000
 I want to talk about the application of option pricing models in valuation.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.000
 Not to value options, that's been around a while, but to value real businesses.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.000
 What kind of businesses?

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.000
 It could be an oil company with undeveloped reserves.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.000
 A young biotechnology company.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.000
 Equity in a deeply troubled company.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.000
 A company losing money with a lot of debt.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:46.000
 This session I hope to flesh out why I think of investments in these businesses as options

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.000
 and what the implications are for investors.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.000
 So now that we've talked about intrinsic and relative valuation,

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:57.000
 it's time to turn our attention to the third and final approach to doing valuation.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.000
 Real options.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:03.000
 As I noted at the very start of the class, this is perhaps the only area of valuation

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.000
 where we can draw new and different things.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.000
 Quasi-sophisticated models to do valuation.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:15.000
 But in this session I'd like to cut to the core and talk about the intuition that drives real option valuation.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.000
 When you do a discounted cash flow valuation, you take the expected cash flows and you discount them back

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.000
 and you come up with a value for the asset, right?

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:26.000
 Well, for most assets, that is an appropriate measure of value.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:31.000
 But you could argue that in some cases you're going to underestimate the value of an asset,

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:35.000
 especially when there are the following options embedded in the asset.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:38.000
 The first is the option to delay.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.000
 An investment that looks bad today might become good tomorrow

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:45.000
 and having the proprietary rights to the investment can still be valuable.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.000
 The second is you have the option to expand.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:53.000
 You can have an investment that does not look good today in terms of cash flows,

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:59.000
 but it might give you a chance to enter a new market or create a new product that is incredibly valuable.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.000
 That is the option to expand.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.000
 And the third is the option to abandon.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:10.000
 In some investments you might get the right or the option to walk away from that investment if things don't go well.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.000
 That is the option to abandon.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:17.000
 Generically speaking, we're saying that if you have an asset with these options embedded in them,

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:22.000
 traditional discounted cash flow valuation is going to understate the value of these assets.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:31.000
 In fact, when you use option pricing in valuing businesses, you're arguing for attaching a premium to traditional discounted cash flow valuations.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:39.000
 So it's good to be clear, option pricing valuation is not an alternative to discounted cash flow valuation, it's an augmentation.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:45.000
 You first have to do a discounted cash flow valuation before you embark on option pricing.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:51.000
 To give you an idea of where the value of an option comes from, let me give you a very simple illustration.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:03:00.000
 Let's assume you have an investment where there's a 50% chance you could make 100 million and a 50% chance you could lose 120 million.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.000
 The expected value of this investment is negative, right?

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:05.000
 You would not take this investment.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:09.000
 Now let's say I took the same investment and broke it down into two steps.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.000
 In the initial step, you take it in a smaller increment.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.000
 So in that first step, you get one of two outcomes.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:19.000
 Either the investment comes back as a good investment, in which case you make plus 20 million.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:22.000
 Or it comes back as a bad investment, in which case you lose 20 million.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:27.000
 If you lose the 20 million, you stop the investment right away.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.000
 But if you make the 20 million, you continue.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:34.000
 And if you win, you make another 80 million, giving you a total upside of 100 million.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:40.000
 And if you lose, you lose another 100 million, giving you a total downside of 120 million.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:45.000
 If you look at the probabilities, this investment is actually equivalent to the first investment.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.000
 There's a 50% chance that you'll make 100 million.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:53.000
 And there's a 50% chance, cumulatively, that you lose 120 million.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:55.000
 But here's the magic of options.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:01.000
 If you take the expected value of this investment, you're actually going to end up with a positive expected value.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:06.000
 A bad investment became a good investment when you took it in two steps.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:12.000
 Now step back and think about why the second investment was valuable and the first investment was not.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:17.000
 The first aspect that made the second investment more valuable is you got that first try.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:23.000
 You were able to observe what happened in that first try, learn from it, and adapt your behavior.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:28.000
 In fact, those are the two key words that drive the value of real options.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:31.000
 It's learning and adaptive behavior.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:34.000
 Let me stop being abstract and give you a real-world example.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:37.000
 Let's suppose you have to value an oil company.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:41.000
 In a traditional discounted cash flow model, here's what you do.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:45.000
 You take the expected number of barrels of oil that will be produced each year.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.000
 You multiply by an expected oil price.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:49.000
 You come up with an expected cash flow.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:51.000
 And you discount back at a risk-adjusted rate.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:53.000
 What are you missing when you do that?

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:59.000
 If you actually ran an oil company, you would not produce the same number of barrels of oil every year.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:00.000
 And here's why.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.000
 You get to observe the oil price first, right?

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:05.000
 If oil prices are high, you might produce a lot of oil.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:08.000
 If oil prices are low, you might cut back on production.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:11.000
 You have the option to adjust production.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:18.000
 There's learning from looking at the oil price and adaptive behavior because it changed the production that you have based on that price.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.000
 That's what you're looking for in real options.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:23.000
 Is there a capacity to learn?

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:28.000
 And can I change my behavior to make a business or asset more valuable?

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:36.000
 So here are the three basic questions that I'd like to answer when I think about applying option pricing to value businesses.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:39.000
 First, when is there an option in a decision?

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:42.000
 When should I even be talking about option pricing?

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:43.000
 So let's start with that question.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:48.000
 Second question, when does that option have significant economic value?

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:53.000
 And this is where you're going to see a drop-off in the number of options that you can actually value.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:59.000
 Most options that you see out there have either no value or so little value that it's not worth doing this.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:02.000
 So when does that option have significant economic value?

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:06.000
 And the third and final question is, when can I use an option pricing model?

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.000
 Those models that have been developed over the last 40 years to value that option.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:12.000
 So let's start with the first question.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:14.000
 When is there an option embedded in an action?

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:16.000
 When should I be using option pricing?

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:22.000
 There are three specific characteristics that I look for to identify something as an option.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:25.000
 First, options are derivative securities.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:27.000
 They derive their value from something else.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:29.000
 So there's got to be an underlying asset.

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:33.000
 Second, options have contingent payoffs.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:37.000
 Something has to happen for your cash flow to pay off.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:39.000
 And third, options have limited lives.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:43.000
 An underlying asset, a contingent payoff, and limited lives.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:50.000
 In fact, the best way to recognize when you're dealing with an option is to draw the payoff diagram for your cash flows.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:56.000
 And if your payoff diagram looks like an option/payoff diagram, you have an option on your hands.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:02.000
 So very quickly, let's review the two types of option/payoff diagrams you can face.

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:06.000
 If you have a call option, you get the right to buy an asset at a fixed price.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:08.000
 Here's what your payoff diagram will look like.

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:10.000
 There's a kink at the strike price.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:15.000
 And if your value of the asset exceeds the strike price, dollar for dollar you make profits.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:19.000
 But if the value of the asset falls below the strike price, you don't lose an unlimited amount.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:21.000
 You lose what you paid for the option.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:27.000
 So you have limited losses below the strike price, potentially unlimited profits above the strike price.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:32.000
 If you have a put option, it's like holding a mirror up to those same cash flows.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:35.000
 If the value falls below the strike price, now you lose money.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:39.000
 Not an unlimited amount because your price might not be able to drop below zero.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:44.000
 But if the value exceeds the strike price, you lose what you paid for the put.

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:46.000
 So here's how I use payoff diagrams.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:49.000
 And in the sessions following, you're going to see this happen.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:57.000
 Whenever I talk about a real option, I'm going to first draw the payoff diagram to see if, in fact, I have a call or a put option on my hands.

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:01.000
 And once I do that, I'm on my way to using option pricing.

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:07.000
 Second question you need to ask, when is there significant economic value to this option?

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:12.000
 Let me give you the key word that I think drives the discussion of real options.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:14.000
 It's exclusivity.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:19.000
 If you and only you can exercise this option, this option has significant value.

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:24.000
 The less exclusivity you have, the less value there is to the option.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:29.000
 Again, this might sound mysterious, but let me give you a very quick anecdote to bring this home.

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:34.000
 A few years ago, a second-year MBA came into my office, and he was very excited.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:42.000
 His landlord had given him, he said, an option to buy the apartment he was renting, and he wanted to use an option pricing model to value the option.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:47.000
 I said, "Okay. What price did he say you could buy this apartment at?"

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:52.000
 The MBA student thought for a while, and he said, "You know what? He never mentioned a price."

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:54.000
 So I said, "Let's get this straight.

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:59.000
 Your landlord has told you you can buy the apartment you're renting right now, anytime over the next year,

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:01.000
 for whatever the prevailing market price is, right?"

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:03.000
 And he said, "Hey, I guess that's what I've got."

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:05.000
 I said, "What do you think that's worth?"

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:08.000
 Anybody can buy that apartment at that market price.

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:10.000
 You have no exclusivity.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:12.000
 You have no option value.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:15.000
 So with every real option, this is a question we'll stop and ask.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:17.000
 Is there exclusivity?

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:19.000
 And it's not a zero-one proposition.

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:22.000
 If you have total exclusivity, the total value of the option will count.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:26.000
 If you have absolutely no exclusivity, there is no option value.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:30.000
 If you're somewhere in the middle, you get part of the value of the option.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:37.000
 Now, once you decide your option is exclusivity, then we know what the determinants of option value are, and there are only six.

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:39.000
 Three relate to the underlying asset.

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:41.000
 One is the value of the underlying asset.

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:44.000
 As that moves up and down, the value of your option will change.

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:48.000
 The second is the variance in that value.

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:52.000
 As that variance goes up, your options will become more valuable.

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:56.000
 And this is where asset pricing gets turned on its head.

00:09:56.000 --> 00:10:00.000
 Because up until now, whenever we've talked about risk, we've been very clear.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.000
 As risk goes up, value goes down in a discounted cash flow model.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:07.000
 As risk goes up, multiples go down in a relative valuation.

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:11.000
 But in an option pricing model, as risk goes up, value goes up.

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:12.000
 And the reason is simple.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:14.000
 You're protected on the downside.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:16.000
 Remember those payoff diagrams?

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:19.000
 You cannot lose more than what you paid for the option.

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:21.000
 So variance and risk becomes your ally.

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:29.000
 And the third and final characteristic relating to the underlying asset that matters is if that asset pays a dividend, it can affect the value of your asset.

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:36.000
 If you have a call option on a stock or an asset that pays a dividend, on the day the dividend is paid, the value of the asset is going to drop,

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:40.000
 which is going to make call options less valuable and put options more valuable.

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:43.000
 There are two variables relating to the option that matter.

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:45.000
 One is the exercise price itself.

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:47.000
 As that changes, the value of the option will change.

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:53.000
 The right to buy something at a fixed price becomes more valuable at a lower fixed price.

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:55.000
 And the other is the life of the option.

00:10:55.000 --> 00:10:59.000
 The more time I give you to play the option, the more valuable it becomes.

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:04.000
 There's only one macro variable that enters the option pricing model, and that's the level of interest rates.

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:06.000
 And it matters for a simple reason.

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:12.000
 When interest rates are high, the present value of what I have to pay in the future, remember the price is fixed, becomes lower.

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:18.000
 So call options become more valuable at higher interest rates and put options become less valuable.

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:26.000
 So once you have an option and you decide that that option has significant economic value, we know the variables that drive the value of the option.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:30.000
 So let's assume you found an option and you've decided it has significant economic value.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:36.000
 The next question and final question you face is can I use an option pricing model to value this option?

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:39.000
 And here we've got to understand the basics of option pricing models.

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:48.000
 I won't bore you with the details, but here are the two basic principles that govern how we use option pricing models or what drives option pricing models.

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:50.000
 The first is the principle of replication.

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:52.000
 What is replication?

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:58.000
 You can replicate or you can create a portfolio of the underlying asset, neither borrowing or lending,

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:02.000
 that is exactly the same cash flows as the option.

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:06.000
 And once you do that, the second principle comes into play, which is arbitrage.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:12.000
 If the option in the replicating portfolio have exactly the same cash flows, they have to trade at the same price.

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:16.000
 So all option pricing models are built on replication and arbitrage.

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:17.000
 But step back.

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:21.000
 To be able to do replication and arbitrage, here are the things you have to be able to do.

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:24.000
 You have to be able to buy and sell the underlying asset.

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:26.000
 You have to be able to buy and sell the option.

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:29.000
 You have to be able to borrow and lend at the risk-free rate.

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:34.000
 Now it's difficult to meet all three conditions, but the further away you get from those three conditions,

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:40.000
 the less likely it is that option pricing models will deliver a fair estimate of value for your option.

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:46.000
 Now with that set up, let me lay out the two basic option pricing models you might run into in practice.

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:49.000
 The first, of course, is the Black-Scholes model.

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:52.000
 The model that invented option pricing, as we know it.

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:58.000
 In the Black-Scholes model, we make restrictive assumptions about a number of variables.

00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:01.000
 For instance, we assume that options are European options.

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:03.000
 What are European options?

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:06.000
 European options can be exercised only at expiration.

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:11.000
 We assume that the variance of the underlying asset remains fixed over the life of the option.

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:16.000
 And finally, we assume that the prices of the underlying asset don't have any jumps to them.

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:17.000
 They're continuous.

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:19.000
 They move in small increments.

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:24.000
 They're big assumptions, but if you make those assumptions, you end up with a very simple model.

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:26.000
 Simple in terms of the inputs you need.

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:32.000
 In the option pricing model, at least as seen by Black-Scholes, there are only six inputs that drive the value of an option.

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:34.000
 The value of the underlying asset.

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:35.000
 The strike price.

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:37.000
 The life of the option.

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:38.000
 The riskless rate.

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:40.000
 The time to expiration.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:42.000
 And the variance in the value of the underlying asset.

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:43.000
 That's it.

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:45.000
 There are no external variables.

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:48.000
 So of those variables, you can value any option.

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:51.000
 But it does base it on restrictive assumptions.

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:53.000
 The alternative is the binomial model.

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:56.000
 In the binomial model, you're less restrictive in your assumptions.

00:13:56.000 --> 00:13:57.000
 You can have early exercise.

00:13:57.000 --> 00:14:00.000
 You can even have variances changing over time.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:01.000
 But here's the catch.

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:07.000
 A binomial model requires you to be able to specify the prices at every branch of the binomial model.

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:10.000
 A lot more information needed to use the binomial model.

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:19.000
 Now, if you look at real options, and especially at real options books, many of them emphasize the fact that the Black-Scholes model is ill-suited to value most real options.

00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:20.000
 They're right.

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:25.000
 Most real options have changing variances over time and require early exercise.

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:32.000
 But having agreed on those terms, I still think that using the binomial model is not an easy choice.

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:37.000
 All of the information you need for the binomial will often lead you into a dead end.

00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:47.000
 And if you can estimate the entire binomial model, I would argue that there's a far simpler way, using decision trees and basic statistics, that you can value any asset.

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:48.000
 So let me put it this way.

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:54.000
 If you have the information to draw the entire binomial model, you don't need option pricing to value an asset.

00:14:54.000 --> 00:15:00.000
 If you don't have that information, you're going to be stuck with the Black-Scholes, notwithstanding its limitations.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:01.000
 So let me sum up.

00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:08.000
 Options are a useful tool to have, but to apply them in valuation, there are three basic tests you've got to meet.

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:12.000
 First, you've got to make sure that there is an option embedded in an action.

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:13.000
 Check for that.

00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:15.000
 Second, check to see if you have exclusivity.

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:18.000
 If you have exclusivity, you have significant option value.

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:29.000
 And third, check to see if you can trade on the underlying asset and the option, because only then can the option pricing models deliver an accurate estimate of value.

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:34.000
 As we'll see, for every hundred options you run out there, maybe two or three will pass these tests.

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:40.000
 But when they do, it's an interesting way and a useful way to estimate value.

